      "tense eyelid edema",
      "relative afferent pupillary defect",
      "restricted motility in all fields of gaze"
    ],
    "margin": "high",
    "rationale": "The acute orbital congestion with tense eyelid edema, chemosis, restricted motility, and severe vision loss with RAPD indicates compressive optic neuropathy, likely from orbital compartment syndrome. The elevated IOP is secondary to orbital congestion, and fluorescein angiography shows delayed choroidal and retinal filling consistent with ischemia from compression.",
    "rejected": [
      {
        "candidate_id": "R5",
        "why": "Acute angle-closure glaucoma would have a shallow anterior chamber and typically not present with tense eyelid edema, chemosis, and restricted motility; the high IOP is likely secondary to orbital congestion."
      },
      {
        "candidate_id": "R3",
        "why": "Central retinal artery occlusion typically shows a cherry-red spot and not serous retinal detachment or subretinal fluid; the orbital signs are not explained."
      },
      {
        "candidate_id": "R1",
        "why": "Toxic optic neuropathy is usually bilateral, progressive, and lacks acute orbital inflammatory signs and elevated IOP."
      },
      {
        "candidate_id": "R4",
        "why": "Rhabdomyolysis is a systemic condition and does not explain the ocular findings; it is a secondary finding."
      }
    ],
